WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.740
The gorilla is something of a paradox on the African scene.
在非洲这片天地里，大猩猩可以说是个矛盾体。

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.630
One thinks one knows him very well.
人们以为自己对它非常了解。

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:13.060
For a hundred years or more he has been killed, captured and imprisoned in zoos.
一百多年来，大猩猩一直遭人猎杀、捕捉，被囚禁在动物园里。

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:23.290
His bones have been mounted in natural history museums everywhere, and he has always exerted a strong fascination upon scientists and romantics alike.
各地的自然历史博物馆里都装架陈列着它的骨骼；无论对科学家还是对富于浪漫幻想的人，它也一向都有着强烈的吸引力。

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:33.790
He is the stereotyped monster of horror films and adventure books, and an obvious (though not perhaps strictly scientific) link with our ancestral past.
它是恐怖片和冒险读物中模式化的怪物形象，也是我们与远祖时代之间一条显而易见的纽带（尽管严格说来，这种联系或许并不科学）。

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.390
Yet the fact is we know very little about gorillas.
然而事实是，我们对大猩猩知之甚少。

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:52.300
No really satisfactory photograph has ever been taken of one in a wild state; no zoologist, however intrepid, has been able to keep the animal under close and constant observation in the dark jungles in which it lives.
至今还没有人拍到过一张真正令人满意的野生大猩猩照片；动物学家无论多么勇敢无畏，都没能在大猩猩栖身的幽暗丛林里密切而持续地观察它。

00:00:53.100 --> 00:01:16.200
Carl Akeley, the American naturalist, led two expeditions to Uganda in the 1920s and now lies buried there among the animals he loved so well; but even he was unable to discover how long the gorilla lives, or how or why it dies, nor was he able to define the exact social pattern of the family groups, or indicate the final extent of their intelligence.
美国博物学家卡尔·埃克利在20世纪20年代曾两次率领考察队前往乌干达，如今就长眠在那里，与他深爱的动物们为伴；但即便是他，也没能查明大猩猩能活多久、怎样死去或为何死去，也没能界定大猩猩家庭群体确切的社会模式，或指明它们的智力最终能达到何种程度。

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:27.370
All this and many other things remain almost as much a mystery as they were when the French explorer Du Chaillu first described the animal to the civilized world a century ago.
所有这一切以及其他许多事情，至今几乎仍和一个世纪前法国探险家杜·沙伊鲁首次向文明世界描述这种动物时一样，是个谜。

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:34.450
The Abominable Snowman who haunts the imagination of climbers in the Himalayas is hardly more elusive.
就连萦绕在喜马拉雅山登山者想象中的雪怪，也几乎不比大猩猩更难以捉摸。

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.020
The little that is known about gorillas certainly makes you want to know more.
关于大猩猩已知的那一点点情况，无疑会让人想知道得更多。

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:48.840
Sir Julian Huxley has recorded that thrice in the London Zoo he saw an eighteen-month-old specimen trace the outline of its own shadow with its finger.
朱利安·赫胥黎爵士曾记述，他在伦敦动物园三次看到一只十八个月大的大猩猩用手指描画自己影子的轮廓。

00:01:49.640 --> 00:02:01.290
“No similar artistic initiative,” he writes, “has been recorded for any other anthropoid, though we all know now that young chimpanzees will paint ‘pictures’ if provided with the necessary materials.”
他写道：“在其他任何类人猿身上，都还没有记录到类似的自发艺术行为，不过如今我们都知道，只要给幼年黑猩猩提供必要的材料，它们就会画‘画’。”

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:11.290
Huxley speaks too of a traveler seeing a male gorilla help a female up a steep rock-step, and gallantry of that kind is certainly not normal among animals.
赫胥黎还提到，有位旅行者看见一只雄性大猩猩扶一只雌性大猩猩登上一级陡峭的岩阶；这种殷勤体贴之举在动物中无疑并不寻常。

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.250
It is this “human-ness” of the gorilla that is so beguiling.
正是大猩猩身上的这种“人性”，才如此令人着迷。

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:20.720
According to some observers he courts and makes love the same way as humans do.
据一些观察者说，大猩猩求爱和交配的方式与人类相同。

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.340
Once the family is established it clings together.
家庭一旦组建起来，成员之间就会紧紧相依。

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:49.240
It feeds in a group in the thick bamboo jungles on the mountainside in the daytime, each animal making a tidy pile of its food—wild celery, bamboo shoots, and other leaves—and squatting down to eat it; and by night each member of the family makes its own bed by bending over and interlacing the bamboo fronds so as to form a kind of oval-shaped nest which is as comfortable and springy as a mattress.
白天，全家在山坡上茂密的竹林里一起觅食，每只大猩猩都把自己的食物——野芹菜、竹笋和其他叶子——整整齐齐地堆成一堆，再蹲下来吃；到了夜里，每个家庭成员都会给自己搭床：把竹子的枝叶压弯、交织起来，形成一种椭圆形的窝，像床垫一样舒适而富有弹性。

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:58.680
The father tends to make his bed just a foot or two from the ground, the mother a little higher, and the children are safely lodged in the branches up above.
父亲往往把床搭在离地面仅一两英尺的地方，母亲的床稍高一些，孩子们则安稳地待在上方的树枝间。

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:07.700
When he walks, the gorilla takes the main weight on his short legs and rests lightly on the knuckles of his hands at the end of his very long arms.
大猩猩走路时，主要由两条短腿承重，而它的两臂很长，臂端的双手以指关节轻轻撑地。

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:16.700
When he stands upright a full-grown male rises to six feet, but with that immense chest he is far heavier than any normal man could ever be.
成年雄性大猩猩站直时，身高达六英尺，但它胸膛那样硕大，体重之大远非任何正常男子所能及。

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.840
Six hundred pounds is not uncommon.
六百磅的体重并不少见。

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.410
His strength is incredible—certainly great enough to take a man in his arms and wrench his head off.
它的力气大得令人难以置信，肯定足以把一个人搂在怀里，把他的脑袋拧下来。

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:38.810
Gorillas appear to talk to one another in high-pitched voices, not unlike those of women, or by smacking their lips or striking their cheeks, and the female, if alarmed, will scream.
大猩猩似乎会用尖细的嗓音（与女人的嗓音颇为相似），或以咂嘴、拍打脸颊的方式彼此交谈；雌性大猩猩受到惊吓时会尖叫。

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:44.030
The male is capable of making a frightening demonstration in the face of danger.
面对危险时，雄性大猩猩能做出令人胆寒的示威举动。

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:50.500
He stays behind while his family gets away, rising to his feet and uttering a terrifying roar.
家人逃走时，它会留在后面，站起身来，发出骇人的咆哮。

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:57.620
Sometimes he will drum on his chest and shake the trees around him with every appearance of uncontrollable fury.
有时，它还会捶打胸膛，摇撼周围的树木，看上去完全是一副怒不可遏的样子。

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.180
In extremity he will charge.
到了危急关头，它会猛冲过来。

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:15.980
But all this is no more than shadow boxing as a general rule, for the gorilla is a gentle, kindly creature, a most forgiving ape who lives at peace with all the other animals, and his reputation for savagery and belligerence is nothing but a myth.
但一般说来，这一切不过是虚张声势，因为大猩猩是一种温和、善良的动物，是非常宽容的猿类，与其他所有动物都和平相处；它那凶残好斗的名声纯属虚构。

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.610
When the animal charges, the thing to do is to stand your ground and look him in the eye.
大猩猩冲过来时，你该做的就是寸步不让，直视它的眼睛。

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.570
Then he will turn aside and slip away through the undergrowth.
这时，它就会转向一旁，穿过灌木丛悄悄溜走。
